---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
const snippet0 = `from power_pptx import Presentation
from power_pptx.animation import Entrance, Exit, Emphasis, MotionPath, Trigger
from power_pptx.util import Inches

prs = Presentation()
slide = prs.slides.add_slide(prs.slide_layouts[5])   # Title Only
slide.shapes.title.text = "Animated demo"
shape = slide.shapes.add_text(
    Inches(1), Inches(2), Inches(4), Inches(1), text="Hello"
)

Entrance.fade(slide, shape)                                    # default: ON_CLICK
Entrance.fly_in(slide, shape, direction="left")                # top/bottom/left/right
Entrance.wipe(slide, shape, direction="top")                   # wipes take one too
Emphasis.pulse(slide, shape, trigger=Trigger.WITH_PREVIOUS)
Exit.wipe(slide, shape, trigger=Trigger.AFTER_PREVIOUS, delay=200)   # delay in ms
Exit.wheel(slide, shape)                                       # also Exit.random_bars

Entrance.fly_in(slide, shape, direction="sideways")            # ValueError

# Motion path preset, or a path of your own
MotionPath.arc(slide, shape, dx=Inches(2), dy=Inches(0), height=0.4)
MotionPath.custom(slide, shape, "M 0 0 L 0.5 0.5 L 1 0 E")     # E ends the path
MotionPath.svg(slide, shape, "M 0 0 H 100 V 50", viewbox=(0, 0, 200, 100))`;

const snippet1 = `# Reveal a text frame one paragraph per click, in order
body = slide.shapes.add_textbox(Inches(1), Inches(3.4), Inches(8), Inches(2))
body.text_frame.text = "First point\\nSecond point\\nThird point"

Entrance.fade(slide, body.text_frame, by_paragraph=True)`;

const snippet2 = `badge = slide.shapes.add_text(
    Inches(6.5), Inches(1.2), Inches(2.5), Inches(0.6), text="NEW"
)

# First effect fires on click; the rest chain with AFTER_PREVIOUS
with slide.animations.sequence():
    Entrance.fade(slide, slide.shapes.title)
    Entrance.zoom(slide, badge)
    Emphasis.pulse(slide, badge)`;

const snippet3 = `from power_pptx.enum.presentation import MSO_TRANSITION_TYPE

# Deck-wide default first...
prs.set_transition(kind=MSO_TRANSITION_TYPE.FADE, duration=400)   # milliseconds

# ...then override individual slides (Morph is a first-class kind)
slide.transition.kind = MSO_TRANSITION_TYPE.MORPH
slide.transition.duration = 1500

prs.save("animated.pptx")`;

---

<DocsLayout
  title="Animations & Transitions"
  description="Entrance, exit and emphasis presets, motion paths, per-paragraph reveal, sequencing, and deck-wide transitions including Morph."
>
  <p class="lead">
    power-pptx exposes PowerPoint's animation model from Python: preset effects, motion paths,
    per-paragraph reveal, and a sequencing context manager — plus per-slide and deck-wide
    transitions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="animations">Animations</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Experimental.</strong> The animation XML is valid against the OOXML schema, survives a
    save and reopen, and imports into LibreOffice as the effect each preset names — but playback
    in Microsoft PowerPoint has not been verified, and has been reported to show animated shapes
    faint and then snap them into view. Prefer slide transitions where they serve, and check an
    animated deck in PowerPoint before relying on it.
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet0} />
  <p>
    <code>direction</code> is one of <code>"bottom"</code>, <code>"top"</code>,
    <code>"left"</code> and <code>"right"</code> for the presets that have one (fly in and out,
    wipe); anything else raises <code>ValueError</code>. Exit effects hide the shape when they end.
    A raw motion path knows <code>M</code>, <code>L</code>, <code>C</code>, <code>Z</code> and
    <code>E</code> (lower case for relative coordinates, as fractions of the slide);
    <code>custom</code> adds a missing closing <code>E</code>, and <code>svg</code> also takes
    <code>H</code>, <code>V</code> and <code>Q</code>, writing quadratic curves as cubics. A
    malformed path raises <code>ValueError</code>, and float durations and delays are rounded to
    whole milliseconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Effects authored in PowerPoint are part of <code>slide.animations</code> too, and new effects
    join PowerPoint's main sequence. <code>shape.delete()</code> and <code>group.ungroup()</code>
    remove the timing entries that pointed at the removed shape, and
    <code>slide.animations.purge_orphans()</code> does the same for anything else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="paragraph-reveal">Per-paragraph reveal</h2>
  <Code code={snippet1} />

  <h2 id="sequencing">Sequencing</h2>
  <Code code={snippet2} />

  <h2 id="transitions">Transitions</h2>
  <Code code={snippet3} />
  <p>
    A deck-wide <code>kind</code> goes only to slides without a kind of their own (pass
    <code>force=True</code> to overwrite those too), while <code>duration</code> and the advance
    settings apply to every slide — so set a slide's own duration after the deck-wide call. A slide
    whose transition carries only timing settings has no kind yet and does take the deck-wide one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>MSO_TRANSITION_TYPE</code> has the ISO set (<code>FADE</code>, <code>PUSH</code>,
    <code>WIPE</code>, <code>COMB</code>, <code>CUT</code>, …), the PowerPoint 2010
    <code>p14</code> kinds (<code>VORTEX</code>, <code>SWITCH</code>, <code>GALLERY</code>,
    <code>CONVEYOR</code>, <code>FLY_THROUGH</code>) and PowerPoint 2016's <code>MORPH</code>
    (<code>p159</code>). Extension kinds are written inside <code>mc:AlternateContent</code> with a
    plain fallback, as PowerPoint writes them, so older viewers degrade gracefully. A transition
    with no member — PowerPoint 2013's Airplane, Curtains, Origami, Wind and the rest — reads as
    <code>kind = None</code> and is saved exactly as it was read unless you change it. Assigning
    <code>None</code> to the last timing setting removes the transition element rather than
    leaving an empty one.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
